diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4d990c0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,358 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Bots are chat members that post messages over HTTP. They're aimed at shell scripts and CI jobs:
+a deploy script can announce that it finished, a test run can post its summary.
+
+## Model
+
+- `Bot` (`bots` table) holds the bot's **code**, the credential that authenticates its requests, and its
+  **creator** (the person who set it up).
+- Each bot has its own `User` (`bots.user_id`, one-to-one). That user carries the bot's name and avatar and is what
+  messages, memberships and notifications point at. So a bot is an ordinary member as far as the rest of the app is
+  concerned: it's named and avatared like anyone, shows as the author of its messages, joins open rooms only when it's
+  set to (see [Open rooms](#open-rooms)), can be granted closed rooms like anyone, and triggers push notifications the
+  same way.
+- The bot's user has no email address and a random password nobody knows. Bots never sign in, but the users table
+  requires a password digest.
+- Deleting a bot (`User#deactivate`, which the bot's delete button and the account members list both use) deactivates
+  its user, the same as for people, and destroys the `Bot` row. That removes the code, so the bot can't post, but its
+  past messages stay attributed to it.
+- Bots are listed as members in the account's people list, the room member pickers and direct-message pickers, as
+  people are. Removing a bot from the people list deactivates it, the same as deleting it on `/bots`. Changing a bot's
+  role there has no effect, since bots never sign in.
+- A bot can be suspended for flooding or for failing webhooks. It keeps everything it has, but can't post or get
+  webhooks until an administrator reactivates it (see [Misbehaving bots](#misbehaving-bots)).
+
+Permissions reuse `User#can_administer?(record)`, which already lets a record's creator or an administrator manage it.
+Any member can create bots, up to the account's limit (see [Limits per member](#limits-per-member)). The list shows
+your own bots, or every bot for administrators.
+
+## Web UI
+
+- `/account/edit` has a bot icon link ("Set up chat bots") for every member, not only administrators.
+- `/bots` lists the bots (no pagination). Each item shows the name, the creator (for administrators), an edit link
+  ("Edit <name>"), and a read-only "Bot code" field with a copy button. The page is marked `turbo-cache-control: no-cache`
+  so Turbo doesn't keep codes in its page cache.
+- `/bots/new` and `/bots/:id/edit` share one form: avatar (optional), name ("Name the bot", required), webhook URL
+  (optional, "Webhook URL", see [Webhooks](#webhooks)), the "Add to all open rooms" switch (off by default, see
+  [Open rooms](#open-rooms)), "Save changes".
+  The edit page adds "Delete this chat bot", which asks for confirmation with Turbo's `data-turbo-confirm`
+  (the native `confirm()`).
+- A suspended bot shows "Suspended" and the reason, to everyone who can see it. Administrators also get a "Reactivate"
+  button, which asks for confirmation with the native `confirm()` (see [Reactivation](#reactivation)).
+- Forms that fail validation re-render with status 422.
+- An empty file field doesn't clear the current avatar. The controller drops blank `avatar` params.
+
+## Limits per member
+
+Administrators can cap how many bots each member can have. The **Bots per member** setting is on `/account/edit`, shown
+only to administrators. It's a whole number, zero or more, and starts at 5.
+
+- A member's count is the bots they created, the same list `/bots` shows them. Deleting a bot destroys its `Bot` row
+  (`User#deactivate`), so deleted bots don't count. Bots other people created don't count either.
+- Suspended bots still count, since they still exist (see [Misbehaving bots](#misbehaving-bots)).
+- Administrators aren't limited: `User#can_add_bots?` is always true for them.
+- At the limit, `/bots` leaves out "Add a chat bot" and says "You’ve reached the limit of N chat bots." `/bots/new`
+  and `POST /bots` redirect back to the list and create nothing.
+- The rule is also a validation on `Bot` (on create only), so every other way of creating a bot is refused too. If two
+  creations race past the controller check, the form re-renders with the same message, with status 422.
+- Lowering the limit never removes or changes existing bots. It only blocks new ones. Raising it again lets members add
+  bots once they're under it.
+- Zero is allowed: members can't create any bots, and administrators still can.
+
+Implementation notes:
+
+- The setting lives in the account's `settings` JSON, next to `restrict_room_creation_to_administrators`. The form posts
+  it as `account[bots_per_member]`, and `AccountsController` permits it at the top level so it always goes through its
+  validation. `settings` itself permits only the keys the form uses.
+- The settings cast would turn `"2.5"` into 2 and `"abc"` into 0, so `Account#bots_per_member=` keeps what was typed and
+  the numericality validation checks that. The typed value is dropped on save and on reload, so validation then sees the
+  stored value.
+- The count and the insert happen in one save transaction. SQLite runs write transactions one at a time
+  (`default_transaction_mode: immediate`), so two creations for the same member can't both see room under the limit.
+  Another database could let one race past it. Locking the creator's row would be the fix then.
+
+## Open rooms
+
+People always join open rooms. Bots don't, unless they're set to. The "Add to all open rooms" switch on the bot form
+(off by default) sets that:
+
+- When it's on, the bot is a member of every open room: the ones that exist when the switch is turned on, and every
+  open room created later. Turning a closed room into an open one counts as creating one.
+- Turning the switch off keeps the bot's current memberships. It only stops the bot joining rooms from then on.
+- The switch never gives a bot a closed room. Those are still granted by a person, in the room's member list, the same
+  as for people.
+- Saving a bot that's already in every open room adds nothing, and tells it nothing.
+- When a bot is added to a room, by the switch or by a person, its webhook gets a `joined` event (see
+  [Webhooks](#webhooks)). A bot is told once per room: one that's removed from a room and added back isn't told again.
+  Which rooms a bot has been told about is kept in `bot_joined_rooms`.
+- Direct conversations never produce a `joined` event, even though a bot can be in one.
+- A join that happens while the bot has no webhook isn't announced, and doesn't count as being told. A bot given its
+  webhook later doesn't hear about the rooms it's already in, but it is told the next time it joins one of them while
+  it has a webhook.
+
+## HTTP API
+
+```
+curl -d "Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅" \
+  https://chat.example.com/rooms/<room-id>/<bot-code>/messages
+```
+
+Route: `POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_code/messages` (`bot_messages_path`), handled by `Bots::MessagesController`.
+
+| Situation                                                     | Status                       |
+|---------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------|
+| Message created                                               | `201 Created`, `Location: /messages/:id` |
+| Unknown, deleted or banned bot code                           | `401 Unauthorized`           |
+| Suspended bot                                                 | `403 Forbidden`              |
+| Cooling down, or the message would go over the limit          | `429 Too Many Requests`, with `Retry-After` in seconds |
+| Room doesn't exist, or the bot isn't a member of it           | `404 Not Found`              |
+| Empty or whitespace-only body and no attachment               | `422 Unprocessable Content`  |
+
+Checks run in that order, so an unknown code gets 401 whatever the room is, and a suspended bot gets 403 whatever the
+room or body is. During a cooldown every attempt gets 429, whatever the room or body is, and each one counts towards
+suspension (see [Misbehaving bots](#misbehaving-bots)). A response never includes the code.
+
+Request bodies:
+
+- **Any content type is taken as plain text.** The raw body is the message, with no JSON envelope and no field names.
+  A body such as `100% done`, `a=b&c=d` or `{not json` is posted as written. Rails doesn't parse it (see below).
+- The text is trimmed, and line breaks (`\r\n` or `\n`) are kept as line breaks.
+- Invalid UTF-8 bytes are replaced with U+FFFD.
+- **Multipart** (`curl -F`) is the one exception: its `attachment` file becomes the message, the way a person's
+  file upload does. A message is either text or a file, as in the web app, so any text in a multipart request is ignored
+  and a multipart request without an attachment is a 422.
+
+Message content: the text is HTML-escaped before it's stored as rich text, so markup in it is shown literally, and
+URLs still autolink like anywhere else. Newlines are stored as `<br>` so they show up in the message.
+
+Once created, the message goes through the same path as a person's: it's broadcast to the room
+(`Message#broadcast_create`), marks the room unread for members, and enqueues `Room::PushMessageJob`.
+
+## Webhooks
+
+A bot can have a **webhook URL** (optional, "Webhook URL" on the bot form; leaving it blank removes the webhook). When a
+message mentions the bot, or the bot is added to a room, Campfire POSTs a JSON description to that URL: a `mention`
+event or a `joined` event. The bot can answer in the HTTP response, or later over the HTTP API.
+
+### When it's called
+
+- A message mentions the bot. The @-mention autocomplete inserts the mention, and that's what triggers the webhook.
+- The bot is active, isn't suspended, has a webhook, and is a member of the room the message was posted to. A bot that
+  has left the room isn't called, and a suspended bot is never called.
+- Messages written by bots never call webhooks. A bot's reply can mention another bot, and that would set off a loop.
+- A message that mentions a bot twice calls it once.
+- Only new messages call webhooks. Editing a message to add a mention doesn't, the same as push notifications.
+- Calls run in `Bot::WebhookJob`, after the message is saved, so the person posting never waits for them. A message
+  deleted before its job runs is dropped.
+- The bot is added to a room, by the open-rooms switch or by a person. Its webhook is called with a `joined` event, once
+  per room (see [Open rooms](#open-rooms)). Direct rooms never produce one. The bot must still be an active member of the
+  room when the call runs, or it isn't called. Calls run in `Bot::JoinedJob`, after the membership is committed.
+
+Each request is a `POST` with `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages" },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": { "text": "restart the build please", "html": "<div class=\"trix-content\">…</div>" },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+- `event` is `mention` or `joined`. More event types may come later, so check it.
+- `room.name` is the room's name. Direct rooms have no name, so they're named after the people in them other than the
+  bot.
+- `message.body.text` is the message as plain text, with the bot's own mention removed. Other people's mentions stay as
+  `@Name`. Removing the mention doesn't tidy the whitespace around it, so "hey @Deploy Bot restart" reads "hey  restart".
+- `message.body.html` is the full rich-text body as the app renders it, sanitized. It still includes the bot's mention.
+- `room.reply_to` is the bot's own posting URL for the room. It's the same path as the HTTP API, so the bot can reply
+  later. **It contains the bot's code**, so whoever receives the payload holds the bot's credential.
+- `message.link` opens the room scrolled to the message.
+- Paths are relative. The bot knows its own Campfire origin.
+
+### The joined event
+
+When the bot is added to a room, its webhook gets:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+- There's no message or author. The room is the one the bot joined, which is never a direct room.
+- `room.name` and `room.reply_to` mean the same as in a mention. `reply_to` contains the bot's code, the same as in a
+  mention.
+
+### Answering
+
+- **`200` with `Content-Type: text/plain`**: the body is posted into the room as the bot's message. It's escaped, and
+  line breaks are kept, the same as text sent over the HTTP API.
+- **`200` with `Content-Type: text/html`**: the body is posted as the bot's rich text. It's cleaned with the filters
+  messages are shown with. Attachments, mentions included, are dropped, so a bot can't ping people.
+- **`204`**: nothing is posted, and nothing is logged.
+- **Any other status, or any other content type on a `200`**: nothing is posted. The response is logged, without the
+  URL or the code.
+- **Empty, or made only of markup**: nothing is posted.
+
+A reply goes into the room the event is about, and only while the bot is still a member of it. A `joined` event can be
+answered the same way as a mention, so a bot can say hello when it joins.
+
+A reply is posted under the same limits as the bot API. It counts towards the message limit, and a reply that's refused
+(during a cooldown, or past the limit) is dropped without an error. See [Misbehaving bots](#misbehaving-bots).
+
+### Delivery rules
+
+- Timeouts: 5 seconds to connect, 10 seconds to write and to read. A bot that needs longer should answer `204` and post
+  later with `reply_to`.
+- The address is checked with `RestrictedHTTP::PrivateNetworkGuard`, the same as link unfurling. `SECURITY.md` treats
+  anything a non-administrator can reach through Campfire as in scope, and a member choosing the URL is exactly that.
+  So loopback, private, link-local and unresolvable addresses are refused.
+- The check happens when a webhook is called, not when the form is saved, since DNS can change. A bot running on
+  `localhost` therefore can't receive webhooks, even in development.
+- The connection goes to the address that was checked, so DNS can't change it in between.
+- Redirects aren't followed.
+- A reply is capped at 1 MB. The body is read in chunks, and an oversized reply is abandoned rather than buffered.
+- Only a `200` reply's body is read. Other responses' bodies aren't.
+- A reply's `charset` is honored. UTF-8 is assumed otherwise. Invalid bytes are replaced.
+- There are no retries. A failed call is logged and dropped. A failed call also counts towards suspending the bot, since
+  ten in a row suspend it (see [Misbehaving bots](#misbehaving-bots)).
+
+### Operating notes
+
+- Webhook jobs (mention and joined) run on the default queue, which push notifications use too. A slow bot holds a worker for up to about 10
+  seconds per call. If that becomes a problem, give webhook jobs their own queue and workers in
+  `config/resque-pool.yml`.
+- Payloads aren't signed, so a receiver can't yet check that a request came from Campfire. Signing (an HMAC over the body
+  with a per-bot secret, say) is the natural next step.
+- The webhook URL is stored as-is, like the code. It's shown only to the bot's creator and to administrators, on the
+  edit page, which is marked `turbo-cache-control: no-cache`. It can carry secrets in its query string, so treat it like
+  the code.
+
+## Misbehaving bots
+
+A bot that floods a room, or whose webhook keeps failing, is suspended automatically. A suspended bot can't post or get
+webhooks, and only an administrator can bring it back.
+
+### Flooding
+
+- A bot may post at most **10 messages in any 10 seconds**. Messages posted through the bot API and webhook replies
+  both count. Only messages that are actually posted count: a refused attempt isn't a message.
+- The window slides. A message counts for 10 seconds from when it was posted, so a bot that posts once a second is never
+  refused.
+- The message that goes over the limit is refused with `429 Too Many Requests` and `Retry-After`, the seconds left, and
+  starts a **30-second cooldown**. A webhook reply that goes over is dropped.
+- During the cooldown, every attempt to post gets 429, whatever the room or body. Webhook replies are dropped. The
+  cooldown doesn't extend, so `Retry-After` counts down to it.
+- **Five attempts during one cooldown suspend the bot.** The fifth attempt still gets 429. From then on the API answers
+  403. The count starts again with each new cooldown.
+
+### Failing webhooks
+
+- A delivery **succeeds on any `2xx`**. Anything else is a failed delivery: another status (a redirect counts, since
+  redirects aren't followed), a timeout, a connection error, or an address that's refused (a private address, or a host
+  that doesn't resolve). A `200` whose reply is too large, or isn't a reply type, is still a success, since the bot
+  answered.
+- A successful delivery resets the count. **Ten failed deliveries in a row suspend the bot.**
+- A delivery that's refused a reply still counts as delivered. Only the delivery itself is counted.
+
+### Suspension
+
+A suspended bot:
+
+- gets `403 Forbidden` from the bot API, before its room or body is checked;
+- gets no webhooks, of either kind, and joins it makes while suspended aren't announced (see
+  [Open rooms](#open-rooms));
+- keeps its rooms, memberships, messages and code, and still counts towards its owner's bot limit;
+- stays on `/bots`, marked "Suspended" with the reason: "Went over its limit of 10 messages in 10 seconds, then kept
+  trying during the 30-second cooldown." or "Its webhook failed 10 times in a row."
+
+The reason is shown to everyone who can see the bot: its creator, and administrators.
+
+### Reactivation
+
+Only administrators can reactivate a suspended bot, from `/bots`, with the "Reactivate" button. Its creator can't. The
+button uses `data-turbo-confirm`, so the browser's native `confirm()` asks first, and cancelling it changes nothing. The
+endpoint is `POST /bots/:bot_id/reactivation` (`Bots::ReactivationsController`).
+
+Reactivating clears the cooldown and both counts, so the bot starts from a clean state. It doesn't clear the message
+window, though. A flooding bot's messages from the last 10 seconds still count, so its next message after reactivation
+may be refused, starting a fresh cooldown. Waiting out the window avoids that. Reactivating a bot that isn't suspended
+does nothing.
+
+### Implementation notes
+
+- State lives on `bots`: `suspended_at` and `suspension_reason` (`Bot::Suspension`), `cooldown_until` and
+  `cooldown_attempts`, and `consecutive_webhook_failures`. The limits are constants in `Bot::Flooding` and
+  `Bot::Suspension`, not settings.
+- Recent messages are counted from the `messages` table, through its `creator_id` index, so there's no second record of
+  posting to keep in step.
+- The window check, the cooldown bookkeeping and the message insert run in one transaction under the bot's row lock
+  (`Bot#post_message`). An API call and a webhook reply can't both see room under the limit. SQLite runs write
+  transactions one at a time anyway. Attachments are processed after the commit, so image work doesn't hold the lock.
+- Refusals are raised after the transaction commits, since raising inside it would roll back the cooldown bookkeeping.
+  The controller turns `Bot::Suspended` into 403 and `Bot::RateLimited` into 429, with `Retry-After`.
+- The cooldown check runs before the room and body are checked (`Bot#refuse_during_cooldown!`, called from
+  `Bots::MessagesController`), so every attempt during a cooldown counts. The webhook path checks inside `post_message`.
+- `Retry-After` is rounded up to whole seconds, so a client that waits that long isn't refused again.
+- Suspended bots are left out of `Bot.with_active_webhook`, which the mention and joined paths both use.
+- A webhook call works on its own copy of the bot. If the bot is deleted while the call is in flight, its counts can't be
+  updated, so the delivery is dropped quietly (`Bot::Webhook#deliver`).
+- Log lines name the bot by id and say why, but never show its code or URL.
+- Tests that travel in time should use whole seconds. `travel_to` drops fractions unless `with_usec: true` is passed.
+
+### Open follow-ups
+
+- Nothing tells the creator that their bot was suspended. Bots don't send notifications yet.
+- Administrators can't see how close a bot is to a limit. A count of recent messages or failures on the bots page would
+  help diagnose a bot before it's suspended.
+
+## Security notes
+
+- **The code is a credential.** It's 24 random base58 characters (`has_secure_token`), unique, and stored as-is,
+  because the list has to show it. Anyone who holds it can post as the bot, so:
+  - It's only shown to the bot's creator and to administrators, on `/bots`. Edit and delete pages don't show it.
+  - It travels in the URL path, which is why it's masked everywhere a path or URL gets recorded:
+    - Request logs: `lib/rails_ext/request_filtered_path.rb` prepends to `ActionDispatch::Request#filtered_path`,
+      and `BotCodeFilter` masks `/rooms/:id/<code>/messages`. Parameter logs mask `code` (`filter_parameter_logging.rb`).
+    - Sentry: `config/initializers/sentry.rb` scrubs the request URL in `before_send`.
+  - Deactivating a user, or banning one, stops its bots from authenticating (`Bot.authenticate` only accepts active users).
+- The endpoint is outside the session and CSRF machinery: `allow_unauthenticated_access`, `skip_forgery_protection`.
+- Rails' parameter parsing is skipped for the body (`Bots::MessagesController#process_action`). Otherwise Rails parses
+  form bodies (`100% done` is an invalid form, so it gets a 400) and JSON bodies (malformed JSON gets a 400). Multipart
+  requests are still parsed, for the attachment.
+- Bot text is HTML-escaped before it's stored, so bots can't inject markup into other people's pages.
+- Webhook payloads carry the code in `room.reply_to`, which is how a bot replies, so a webhook's receiver holds the
+  credential. The webhook's own log lines name the bot by id, never its code or URL. SQL debug logs still show row values
+  (see below).
+- Webhook URLs must be `http` or `https`, and are subject to the same private-address check as link unfurling (see
+  [Webhooks](#webhooks)).
+
+## Known limits and follow-ups
+
+- The code isn't encrypted at rest. The app doesn't configure Active Record encryption keys, and the code has to be
+  shown on the list. Adding encryption would mean adding keys to deployments, and `find_by(code:)` would need
+  deterministic encryption.
+- There's no way to rotate a code yet. Deleting and recreating the bot is the workaround. A "Regenerate code" action
+  is the natural next step.
+- The limits are per bot, and fixed in code. A leaked code can post at the limit (ten messages in ten seconds) until its
+  bot is suspended or deleted.
+- SQL debug logs show the literal values SQLite queries use, including bot codes and session tokens, so don't run
+  production at `RAILS_LOG_LEVEL=debug`.
+- Bot-authored messages don't show a "bot" badge. The author name is the only marker.
+- Neither bot text nor attachments have a size limit, the same as people's messages. A very large body is read
+  into memory in full.
+- There's no `left` event. A bot isn't told when it's removed from a room, so a bot that's removed doesn't know it's
+  no longer in the room until it tries to post there. Like `joined`, it would fit alongside `mention`.
+- System tests need a chromedriver. They didn't run in the sandbox this was built in (it has no network to download
+  one). The UI was checked with controller tests, and by driving a running server in headless Chromium over the DevTools
+  protocol: the form, the composer's autocomplete, posting a mention, and the native `confirm()` on delete. The
+  open-rooms switch was checked the same way, along with a `joined` job running through the async queue adapter after a
+  closed room was created with the bot in it. The suspension flow was checked the same way: a flood over the HTTP API,
+  the bots page's "Suspended" state, and the "Reactivate" confirm, dismissed and then accepted.
diff --git a/lib/bot_code_filter.rb b/lib/bot_code_filter.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8496363
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/bot_code_filter.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,10 @@
+# Bot codes are credentials carried in the path of every bot message (POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_code/messages),
+# so anything that records request paths or URLs has to mask them.
+module BotCodeFilter
+  MASK = "[FILTERED]"
+  PATTERN = %r{(/rooms/[^/?#]+/)[^/?#]+(/messages\b)}
+
+  def self.filter(path_or_url)
+    path_or_url.to_s.gsub(PATTERN, "\\1#{MASK}\\2")
+  end
+end
diff --git a/lib/rails_ext/request_filtered_path.rb b/lib/rails_ext/request_filtered_path.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c8aef85
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/rails_ext/request_filtered_path.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
+# Rails logs request.filtered_path, which only masks query parameters. Bot codes sit in the path, so mask them here too.
+module BotCodeFilteredPath
+  def filtered_path
+    BotCodeFilter.filter(super)
+  end
+end
+
+ActionDispatch::Request.prepend BotCodeFilteredPath
